{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Y9vUpRhdhBExGK9uZ5iWbe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context and determine what needs to be built next.\n\n## Project Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development using classic Unix utilities as a test bed. It derives formal specs from C source code, generates Go implementations from those specs using an AI coding agent, and verifies parity against reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**:\n- `cmd/` - Go implementations of each utility\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `pkg/sys/` - Syscall abstractions (Darwin/Linux)\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting\n\n\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets\n- `docs/` - Specifications, PRDs, use cases, test suites\n\n**Current State**:\n- All specs are written (PRDs, use cases, test suites) for rel01.0 through rel02.1\n- No code has been implemented yet\n- Current focus is rel01.0 (ts utility)\n\n**Roadmap**:\n- rel01.0 (in_progress): ts - Timestamp stdin lines\n  - rel01.0-uc001-ts: in_progress\n- rel01.1 (pending): wc, cat, sponge\n- rel02.0 (pending)\n- rel02.1 (pending): ls extended\n\nThe earliest incomplete release is rel01.0 with the ts utility use case. Looking at what ts actually needs, it depends on pkg/testutils for the differential testing harness and cmd/ts for the implementation itself—pkg/sys and pkg/format aren't required. Following the standard build order of libraries before their consumers, I should start with pkg/testutils, building out the types and interfaces first, then the implementation and tests.\n\nLooking at what pkg/testutils needs to deliver: a test case data structure, binary execution with output capture and timeouts, comparison and reporting logic, normalization hooks that can be composed per-test, and file-state comparison for operations like sponge and cp. This is substantial work, so I'll split implementation from tests, and since DiffTest is the primary exported type, that's what the main file should be named after.\n\nEstimating the scope at 250-350 lines across the data structures, execution logic, comparison, normalization, and file-state handling—this all belongs in a single foundational task since it's the base everything else depends on.\n\nThe types and core functions are small enough to include in the implementation task itself, so I'll combine the struct definitions with the main RunDiffTests function and supporting logic. The test file will be a separate task following the split pattern.\n\nFor file organization, the primary file should be `difftest.go` since DiffTest is the core exported type, with the main execution and normalization logic living there.\n\nNow I need to identify the most impactful single task—implementing pkg/testutils as the foundation for differential testing, since everything else depends on it working correctly. Let me draft the task description with the necessary context from the PRD and architecture docs.\n\nI'm mapping the requirements from prd001-testutils across five major groups: the DiffTest struct and Normalize type, binary execution logic, comparison and reporting, normalization hooks like TimestampNormalizer, and file-state comparison. This covers about 17 sub-requirements total, which fits the target of 5-8 requirements per task.\n\nNow I'm consolidating these into discrete task requirements: implementing the DiffTest struct with its fields, the RunDiffTests function for executing and capturing output with configurable timeouts, comparison logic that normalizes and reports divergences with named subtests, and the normalization hook composition system. R4.3 (slice of Normalize functions applied in order)\nR5: Implement TimestampNormalizer per prd001-testutils R4.2 (replace strftime patterns with fixed placeholder)\nR6: Implement file-state comparison per prd001-testutils R5.1-R5.2 (compare expected file content after execution)\n\nThat's 6 requirements - in range.\n\nAcceptance criteria (5-8):\nAC1: DiffTest struct compiles with all fields from prd001-testutils R1.1-R1.4\nAC2: RunDiffTests executes two binaries with identical args, stdin, and env\nAC3: Comparison reports divergence with triggering input, both outputs, and both exit codes\nAC4: Normalization hooks are applied in order before comparison\nAC5: TimestampNormalizer replaces common strftime patterns with a fixed placeholder\nAC6: File-state comparison detects differing file content after binary execution\nAC7: All code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint\nAC8: No test file is created (tests are a separate task)\n\nActually, let me reconsider AC8 - that doesn't quite fit. The DiffTest struct needs Args, Stdin, Env, expected exit code, Normalize functions, WorkDir, and file expectations. RunDiffTests takes testing.T, two binary paths, and a DiffTest slice, running each as a named subtest. Each subtest executes both binaries identically, capturing stdout, stderr, and exit code, with a configurable timeout defaulting to 10 seconds. seconds) fails the test if either binary exceeds it\nAC5: Normalization functions are applied in order to both outputs before byte-for-byte comparison\nAC6: TimestampNormalizer replaces \"%b %d %H:%M:%S\"-style timestamps with a fixed placeholder string\nAC7: File-state comparison checks expected file content byte-for-byte and reports divergence\nAC8: All pkg/testutils code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint\n\nThat's 8 - in range.\n\nDesign decisions (3-5):\nD1: Primary source file is pkg/testutils/difftest.go (named after the primary exported type DiffTest per P7)\nD2: Normalize field is a slice of func([]byte) []byte, not a single function (per prd001-testutils R4.3)\nD3: Timeout is implemented via context.WithTimeout on exec.CommandContext (per prd001-testutils R2.3)\nD4: Each DiffTest runs as a t.Run subtest named from the test case index or a Name field (per prd001-testutils R3.6)\nD5: File expectations use a map[string][]byte where keys are file paths relative to the working directory (per prd001-testutils R5.1)\n\nThat's 5 - in range.\n\nNow I'm reviewing the PRD more carefully to identify the concrete design choices that should be captured. The function signature is RunDiffTests(t, goBinary, refBinary, tests), and the DiffTest struct needs fields for command arguments, stdin input, environment variables, exit code expectations, and normalization capabilities. The Normalize field should follow the func([]byte) []byte type signature, and timeout handling relies on context-based command execution.\n\nI'm noticing a design tension: R1.1 specifies an optional singular normalization function, but R4.3 calls for accepting a slice of Normalize functions applied sequentially. The slice approach is more flexible and takes precedence. Also, the exit code comparison isn't against a hardcoded expected value—it's a direct comparison between the Go binary's exit code and the reference binary's exit code, as clarified by R3.4 and R3.5.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering what the expected exit code field in R1.1 actually represents. Looking at the acceptance criteria, it seems the field might serve a different purpose—perhaps validating that both binaries produce that specific exit code, or it could be optional when a reference binary isn't available. The core comparison logic still compares the two binaries' exit codes directly.\n\nFor the file structure, P7 is clear: since DiffTest is the primary exported type, the file should be named `difftest.go` in the `pkg/testutils/` directory, not `testutils.go`.\n\nTimestampNormalizer is a function rather than a type, so it can coexist in the same file alongside the other normalizer code without exceeding the 250-350 line budget.\n\nFor the task title, I need to anchor it to the specific PRD requirement IDs as the constraints require, so something like \"Implement pkg/testutils differential testing harness (prd001 R1-R5)\" captures the scope and references the relevant requirements.\n\nNow I'm extracting the concrete design choices directly from the PRD so I don't invent anything: the DiffTest struct needs those specific fields, the Normalize function type signature, the 10-second default timeout that's configurable, support for working directories with temp directory defaults, named subtests via t.Run, the TimestampNormalizer pattern replacement logic, applying multiple normalizers in sequence, specifying expected file content, and the RunDiffTests entry point. I've identified the five key design decisions from the PRD. Now I need to gather the actual file paths for the required reading—the PRD itself is at `docs/specs/product-requirements/prd001-testutils.yaml`, the architecture doc is at `docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml`, and I should include `go.mod` so the agent understands the module structure. Since the implementation status shows the spec is complete but no code exists yet in `pkg/testutils/`, I'll be creating this file from scratch. Implement pkg/testutils differential testing harness (prd001 R1-R5)\n\nI need to define the DiffTest struct with fields for command arguments, stdin, environment variables, exit code expectations, normalization functions, and working directory, along with a Normalize function type that transforms byte slices. The RunDiffTests function will execute both the reference and candidate binaries with identical inputs in subtests, capturing their stdout, stderr, and exit codes independently for comparison.\n\nWhen a test's working directory is empty, I'll create a temporary directory for that specific test. The comparison logic applies any normalization functions in sequence to both outputs before doing byte-for-byte comparisons of stdout and stderr, plus exact exit code matching. On divergence, I'll report the test arguments, truncated stdin, both sets of output, and both exit codes, while passing tests produce no output.\n\nThe normalization system works as a composable slice of functions that transform byte slices in order—if the slice is nil or empty, no normalization happens. I'm also implementing a timestamp normalizer that replaces common time patterns like \"Feb 19 12:34:56\" with a placeholder, which the cmd/ts tests will use. Beyond output comparison, the test framework also supports checking file state after execution, comparing expected file content byte-for-byte and reporting any divergence the same way as stdout mismatches.\n\nFor the API design, the main entry point is a function that takes a testing context, two binary paths, and a slice of test cases—each cmd package just defines its test cases and calls this from a standard test function. Binary execution uses a context with a 10-second default timeout, and the test fails clearly if either binary exceeds it. Each test case runs as a named subtest... utils R3.6 so individual test case failures are identifiable in go test output.\"\n      - id: D5\n        text: \"Primary source file is pkg/testutils/difftest.go, named after the primary exported type DiffTest. The package is testutils, not difftest.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"DiffTest struct compiles with all fields specified in prd001-testutils R1.1-R1.4: Args, Stdin, Env, ExitCode, Normalize slice, WorkDir, and file expectations.\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"RunDiffTests accepts (t *testing.T, goBinary string, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest) and runs each test case as a named subtest.\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"Each subtest executes both binaries with identical args, stdin, env, and working directory, capturing stdout, stderr, and exit codes separately.\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"A configurable timeout (default 10 seconds) fails the test with a clear timeout message if either binary exceeds it.\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"Normalization functions from the Normalize slice are applied in order to both outputs before byte-for-byte comparison of stdout, stderr, and exit codes.\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"TimestampNormalizer replaces strftime-formatted timestamps (e.g., 'Feb 19 12:34:56') with a fixed placeholder so that ts differential tests pass despite wall-clock differences.\"\n      - id: AC7\n        text: \"File-state comparison checks expected file content byte-for-byte after execution and reports divergence in the same format as stdout divergence.\"\n      - id: AC8\n        text: \"All pkg/testutils code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n\nNow I'm verifying the implementation meets all the acceptance criteria and design decisions—the code should compile cleanly without any linter warnings. I need to add a Name field to the DiffTest struct since R3.6 requires named subtests, even though the PRD doesn't explicitly list it—the test suite examples show each case has a name field that would be used for the subtest identifier.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":71783,"cache_read_input_tokens":15594,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":71783},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4d8b62bd-67d5-4574-8838-0606a9c36ca5","uuid":"c9317955-1ed0-4c9a-b87d-0e0a6f8bd8f5"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Y9vUpRhdhBExGK9uZ5iWbe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is a specification-driven development experiment that derives formal specs from C source code of classic Unix utilities, generates Go implementations from those specs via an AI coding agent (mage-claude-orchestrator), and verifies parity against Homebrew GNU reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify) with shared components:\n- `cmd/` — One package per utility (ts, wc, cat, sponge, ls, du, etc.)\n- `pkg/testutils/` — Differential testing harness (foundation for all verification)\n- `pkg/sys/` — Darwin/Linux syscall abstractions (terminal width, file metadata, signals)\n- `pkg/format/` — Output formatting (columns, ANSI color, human-readable sizes)\n- `magefiles/` — Mage build targets bridging human-guided spec and automated synthesis\n\n**Current state**: All specifications are complete (PRDs, use cases, test suites for rel01.0 through rel02.1). **No code has been implemented yet.** The project is at the transition point from specification to synthesis.\n\n**Current release**: rel01.0 (in_progress) — ts utility. Use case rel01.0-uc001-ts is in_progress. This requires pkg/testutils (the differential testing harness) and cmd/ts.\n\n## Priority Analysis\n\nThe earliest incomplete release is **rel01.0** (ts timestamp stdin lines). The dependency chain is:\n\n1. **pkg/testutils** — Must be built first. It is the acceptance gate for every utility. Without it, no differential test can run. ts, wc, cat, sponge, ls, du all depend on it.\n2. **cmd/ts** — Depends on pkg/testutils for verification. Does NOT require pkg/sys or pkg/format.\n\nPer canonical ordering: libraries before consumers → pkg/testutils before cmd/ts. Per P2: implementation and tests are separate tasks.\n\nThe single highest-priority task is implementing the pkg/testutils differential testing harness.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Implement pkg/testutils differential testing harness (prd001 R1-R5)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd001-testutils.yaml (full PRD for pkg/testutils -- DiffTest struct, RunDiffTests API, normalization hooks, file-state comparison, acceptance criteria)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (pkg/testutils component description, design decision DD2 on differential testing)\n      - go.mod (module path and Go version)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/testutils/difftest.go\n        action: create\n        note: DiffTest struct, Normalize function type, RunDiffTests entry point, comparison and divergence reporting, TimestampNormalizer, file-state comparison\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Define a DiffTest struct with fields for: Name (string, used as subtest name), Args ([]string), Stdin ([]byte, nil means EOF immediately per prd001 R1.2), Env ([]string, nil means inherit test process environment per prd001 R1.3), ExitCode (int), and Normalize (a slice of normalize functions per prd001 R4.3). Define the normalize function type as func([]byte) []byte per prd001 R1.4. Include a WorkDir field (string, empty means use a per-test temp directory per prd001 R2.5). Include a FileExpectations field for utilities whose primary output is a file per prd001 R5.1.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary string, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest) that iterates the test slice and runs each DiffTest as a named subtest via t.Run (prd001 R3.6). For each test case, invoke both the Go binary and the reference binary with identical args, stdin, env, and working directory. Capture stdout, stderr, and exit code from each binary independently per prd001 R2.1-R2.2. Impose a configurable timeout on each invocation with a default of 10 seconds; fail the test with a clear timeout message if either binary exceeds it per prd001 R2.3. Produce no output during a passing test run per prd001 R2.4.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement comparison and divergence reporting: apply the Normalize function slice in order to both binaries' stdout and stderr before comparison (prd001 R3.1). Compare normalized stdout byte-for-byte -- any difference is a test failure (prd001 R3.2). Compare normalized stderr byte-for-byte (prd001 R3.3). Compare exit codes exactly (prd001 R3.4). On divergence, report: DiffTest args, stdin content truncated to 256 bytes, reference stdout, Go stdout, reference stderr, Go stderr, and both exit codes (prd001 R3.5).\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Implement normalization hook composition: the Normalize field on DiffTest is a slice of func([]byte) []byte. Each function in the slice is applied in order to the raw output bytes, producing normalized bytes for comparison. When the slice is nil or empty, no normalization is applied per prd001 R4.1, R4.3.\"\n      - id: R5\n        text: \"Implement a TimestampNormalizer function matching func([]byte) []byte that replaces common strftime timestamp patterns -- syslog format ('Feb 19 12:34:56'), HH:MM:SS elapsed format, epoch with microseconds -- with a fixed placeholder string. This normalizer is used by cmd/ts differential tests to eliminate wall-clock divergence per prd001 R4.2.\"\n      - id: R6\n        text: \"Implement file-state comparison for utilities whose primary output is a file (sponge, cp, mv). DiffTest.FileExpectations specifies file paths and their expected content after execution. After both binaries run, compare expected file content byte-for-byte and report divergence in the same format as stdout divergence per prd001 R5.1-R5.2.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"The primary API is RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary string, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest) per prd001-testutils AC1. A cmd/ package defines a []DiffTest slice and calls RunDiffTests from a TestXxx function with no other setup.\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Normalize field on DiffTest is a slice of func([]byte) []byte applied in order per prd001-testutils R4.3. When nil or empty, no normalization is applied. This replaces a single-function design to support composition of multiple normalizers.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Binary execution uses exec.CommandContext with context.WithTimeout. Default timeout is 10 seconds per prd001-testutils R2.3. The test fails with a clear timeout message rather than hanging the test run per prd001 AC5.\"\n      - id: D4\n        text: \"Each DiffTest runs as a named subtest via t.Run using the DiffTest.Name field per prd001-testutils R3.6, so individual test case failures are identifiable in go test output.\"\n      - id: D5\n        text: \"Primary source file is pkg/testutils/difftest.go, named after the primary exported type DiffTest per project convention (P7). The package name is testutils.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"DiffTest struct compiles with all fields specified in prd001-testutils R1.1-R1.4: Name, Args, Stdin, Env, ExitCode, Normalize slice, WorkDir, and FileExpectations.\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"RunDiffTests(t, goBinary, refBinary, tests) runs each DiffTest as a named subtest. A cmd/ package can call it from a TestXxx function with no other setup per prd001 AC1.\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"Each subtest executes both binaries with identical args, stdin, env, and working directory, capturing stdout, stderr, and exit code independently per prd001 R2.1-R2.2.\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"A timeout of 10 seconds (default) fails the test with a clear timeout message if either binary exceeds it per prd001 AC5.\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"Normalization functions from the Normalize slice are applied in order to both outputs before byte-for-byte comparison. A passing test run produces no output per prd001 AC2.\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"TimestampNormalizer replaces strftime-formatted timestamps (e.g., 'Feb 19 12:34:56') with a fixed placeholder so that ts differential tests pass despite wall-clock differences per prd001 AC3.\"\n      - id: AC7\n        text: \"File-state comparison checks expected file content byte-for-byte after execution and reports divergence when the Go binary writes different content than the reference binary per prd001 AC4.\"\n      - id: AC8\n        text: \"All pkg/testutils code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint per prd001 AC6.\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":71783,"cache_read_input_tokens":15594,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":71783},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4d8b62bd-67d5-4574-8838-0606a9c36ca5","uuid":"45968f6e-9bc5-49e1-b1e6-261ce058508b"}
